## Exact compressed word and order

Let a0 be the frozen 24 nominal amplitudes, F(a) the complete template
endpoint, U0=F(a0), and D=U0^n. If one calibrated template F(a) has been
found, emit the chronological schedule

    repeat M times:
        calibrated 24-slot template F(a)
        repeat n-1 times: nominal 24-slot template U0.

Its complete matrix is B(a)^M, where

    B(a)=U0^(n-1)F(a)=D U0^dagger F(a).                       (2)

The adjoint in the second expression is an algebraic identity for checking;
the physical word has only the positive-time schedule in the first
expression. No inverse-time actuator is requested. The proposed serialized
word is an exact repetition tree, one nominal amplitude list, one calibrated
list and the integers M,n-1. Compression does not compress physical time.

## A root with explicit phase intervals and no equality oracle

Because XX and ZZ commute, the four rank-one projectors

    P_(z,x)=(I+zZZ)(I+xXX)/4,  z,x in {+1,-1},

are exact and sum to I. W has eigenvalue w=(-7+24i)/25 for x=+1 and
1 for x=-1. Define

    phi_w=pi/2+2 atan(1/7),     exp(i phi_w)=w.

Indeed atan(4/3)=pi/4+atan(1/7), and the half-angle formula gives
exp(2i atan(4/3))=(3+4i)^2/25. Thus no numerical spectral basis is needed.

Choose integers k and m_(z,x) and certified phase intervals for

    theta=T-2pi k,
    phi_(z,x)=1_(x=+1) phi_w-(2+z)theta-2pi m_(z,x),
    |phi_(z,x)|<4.                                             (3)

Then the exact unitary

    Z=sum_(z,x) P_(z,x) exp(i phi_(z,x)/M)

satisfies Z^M=V_T, including the scalar, and

    ||Z-I||<=max|phi_(z,x)|/M<4/M<r/4.                         (4)

These are open interval certifications, not principal-branch equalities.
A concrete finite construction uses 512-bit dyadic intervals for pi from
Machin's formula 16 atan(1/5)-4 atan(1/239), with 128 terms of each
alternating arctangent series and exact remainder bounds. The identity can
be checked from tan(4 atan(1/5))=120/119 and the subtraction formula with
atan(1/239), together with its quadrant. Use the same 128-term series for
atan(1/7). This is a proposed implementation, not a library precision claim.

Take k as the nearest integer to T/(2 pi_mid), using an exact rational
midpoint, and similarly reduce each small phase by a nearest integer.
Tie choices may be deterministic; no exact transcendental equality must
be decided. Since T<2^220, the absolute phase uncertainty from a pi interval
of width <=2^-508 is far below 2^-280. Form the arctangent partial sums and
remainders exactly, then round the combined pi interval outward to 512-bit
endpoints; the proposed width bound has ample rounding allowance. Rounding to the nearest approximate
multiple leaves a central angle near [-pi,pi], with a generous open margin
to (-4,4). The certificate must actually verify the interval containments
in (3), rather than infer them from an unverified decimal reduction.

For direct comparison, the same theta interval supplies zeta=exp(-iT)
=exp(-i theta). Then exp(-iTK)=diag(zeta^3,zeta,zeta,zeta^3), so V_T has a
direct exact target enclosure independent of a root branch. Taylor order
256 for exp(iu), |u|<=4, has tail bounded by 81*4^257/257!; directed integer
evaluation and this tail suffice for the proposed phase-enclosure stage.
The exact target W is rational, not a floating approximation to (1).

## Conditional bounded calibration lemma

Use the parent's exact rational approximate inverse, here denoted R_inv
to distinguish it from any target root. Its frozen residual fraction gives

    ||I-R_inv A||<=2^-38,    ||R_inv||<=B=63,
    L=4096/225, c=16/15,
    t=25/229376=1/(8BL),     r=t/(16B).

The strengthened 2^-38 bound is from the chart's actual squared residual
intervals, not its weaker nominal acceptance threshold 1/2. It is an explicit
provisional dependency and must survive separate checking.

Write G(x)=coeff Im(U0^dagger F(a0+x)), with the original selected-coordinate
order, and set y=coeff Im(D^dagger Z). The recurrence gives ||D-I||<10^-13,
and (4) gives ||D^dagger Z-I||<r/2, hence ||y||<r/2. Define

    Phi_y(x)=x+R_inv[y-G(x)].                                 (5)

On the radius-t ball,

    ||D Phi_y||<=||I-R_inv A||+B L t
                 <=2^-38+1/8<1/4=:q_bound.

Also ||Phi_y(0)||<=Br/2=t/32. Therefore Phi_y maps that ball within
radius (1/4+1/32)t=9t/32. It has a unique fixed point x*, with ||x*||<t.
R_inv is invertible because R_inv A is invertible, so G(x*)=y. Both
U0^dagger F(a0+x*) and D^dagger Z have positive definite real part; the
parent's imaginary-part uniqueness argument gives

    F(a0+x*)=U0 D^dagger Z,   B(a0+x*)=Z.

Thus one exact local solution suffices for the repeated target word.

There is a bounded numerical supplier proposal for this one solution:
start x0=0 and take exactly 32 approximate iterations of (5). Enclose y
and each G(x_k) with vector 2-norm error <=2^-108; for example require
each of their 16 coordinate radii <=2^-110, and take exact rational
midpoints. Apply the exact rational R_inv and round every new displacement
to a multiple of 2^-128. The rounding vector norm is <=2^-127. Therefore
the error of each implemented map value is at most

    d <=63*(2*2^-108)+2^-127 <2^-100.

Since d<t/32, every iterate remains strictly inside the radius-t ball
(in fact the radius-t/2 ball), and contraction gives

    ||x_32-x*||<=t*4^-32+(4/3)*2^-100.                       (6)

The exact emitted word then satisfies

    ||B(a0+x_32)^M-V_T||
       <=4cM [t*4^-32+(4/3)*2^-100] <2^-45<epsilon.          (7)

For the last inequality it suffices that M<2^28, 4c<5 and t<2^-13:
the first term is <5*2^-49<2^-46, and the second is <2^-69.
No new Jacobian is needed. G(0)=0 is exact, so at most 32 new template
endpoint evaluations suffice: 31 intermediate nonzero iterates and the
final template. The actual program must certify the widths and domain at
every stage; this plan does not assert those executions already exist.

The selected-coordinate precision is distinct from the old nominal repeat
precision. Parameter-fit/rounding error in the varied template is repeated
M times, not Mn times. In contrast a physical fault in every nominal
template is repeated Mn times and needs the separate full-duration budget.

## Minimal final acceptance test, independent of solver success rhetoric

The additional certificate should contain the 16 exact dyadic displacements,
the phase-reduction integers/intervals, calibrated full template enclosure,
complete schedule tree and a direct whole-word residual. The finite iteration
log shows how the witness was obtained; final acceptance should primarily
use the emitted word's full matrix comparison, not a claimed solver tolerance.

Reuse the recurrence's fresh degree-320, scale-2^768 endpoint rectangle
Uhat and power rectangle Dhat=Uhat^n. Their separate analytic errors are

    ||Uhat-U0||<=e0=384*81/2^321,
    ||Dhat-D||<=eta_D=2n e0.

Enclose the final calibrated template Fhat with the same N320/8-step/S768
method, so ||Fhat-F||<=e_a=e0 for amplitudes in the permitted box. Form the
auxiliary interval product Bhat=Dhat Uhat^dagger Fhat. Its exact-product
analytic error from B in (2) is at most the explicit rational

    beta=eta_D(1+e0)(1+e_a)+e0(1+e_a)+e_a.                  (8)

All interval rounding is already enclosed, and is additional to (8).
Binary-power Bhat to M. Since B is unitary, if M beta<1/2 then

    ||Bhat^M-B^M||<=2M beta.                                (9)

The existing parent's eta_D is <10^-35, so this stage has ample analytic
margin for epsilon=2^-40; nevertheless check the actual rational inequality.
No enormous power n needs to be recomputed: Dhat is an immutable input.

Subtract a direct enclosure of V_T from the final power rectangle. If D_res^2
is the sum of squared maximum absolute real and imaginary entry endpoints,
the decisive finite strict test is

    epsilon-2M beta>0,
    D_res^2 < (epsilon-2M beta)^2.                           (10)

The normalized dyadic scale must be included in D_res^2. This tests every
old input and reference, including global phase. It remains valid even if
the proposed contraction implementation has a selection defect, provided
the final enclosure and legal amplitude checks are independently sound.
It is the smallest new substantive certification obligation: an actual
rational calibrated word with (10), not another existence theorem.

